            "text/markdown": "Here's an optimized Python implementation of the Merge Sort algorithm, incorporating improvements and clarity:\n\n```python\ndef merge_sort(arr):\n    if len(arr) <= 1:\n        return arr\n\n    mid = len(arr) // 2\n    left_half = merge_sort(arr[:mid])\n    right_half = merge_sort(arr[mid:])\n    \n    return merge(left_half, right_half)\n\ndef merge(left, right):\n    sorted_array = []\n    left_index, right_index = 0, 0\n    \n    # Compare elements from left and right subarrays\n    while left_index < len(left) and right_index < len(right):\n        if left[left_index] <= right[right_index]:\n            sorted_array.append(left[left_index])\n            left_index += 1\n        else:\n            sorted_array.append(right[right_index])\n            right_index += 1\n    \n    # Extend the sorted_array with any remaining elements\n    sorted_array.extend(left[left_index:])\n    sorted_array.extend(right[right_index:])\n\n    return sorted_array\n\n# Sample usage\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    example_array = [38, 27, 43, 3, 9, 82, 10]\n    sorted_array = merge_sort(example_array)\n    print(\"Sorted array:\", sorted_array)\n```\n\n### Key Points:\n\n1. **Base Case**: If the list contains zero or one element, it is inherently sorted. The function immediately returns it.\n\n2. **Recursion**: The list is divided by finding the middle index, recursively sorting the two halves, and then merging the sorted halves.\n\n3. **Merging**: The `merge` function uses two indices to track and compare elements in the two sorted halves. The `extend` method efficiently appends any remaining elements once one subarray is exhausted.\n\n4. **Efficiency**: This method maintains the \\(O(n \\log n)\\) time complexity of merge sort and intelligently manages space complexity through slicing and temporary storage in the `sorted_array`.\n\nThis version of Merge Sort is concise, easy to understand, and demonstrates the algorithm's principles effectively."
